Award-winning camera app <a href="https://halide.cam/">Halide Mark III</a> has been updated to version 3.2, bringing dedicated support for iPhone 18 Pro by broadening user control over the device's new variable aperture camera system.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/halide-v3-2.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1068077" /><br/>
Apple's stock Camera app lets iPhone 18 Pro owners choose from a four-stop variable aperture ranging from ƒ/1.48 to ƒ/4.0, delivering around 50 percent better low-light performance and giving users more optical control over depth of field.<br/>
<br/>
Halide 3.2 does away with that four-stop limitation and instead lets you select any ƒ-stop setting within the camera's full range. <br/>
<br/>
There are two styles of aperture control in the app. The first is Aperture Priority mode, where you can set the ƒ-stop and the app handles the shutter speed and ISO to properly expose the shot.<br/>
<br/>
The other style is Manual mode, which gives users full control over all three settings. This includes ISO settings – something that Apple's stock Camera app doesn't offer manual control over. Halide has said it is also considering adding f-stop presets in future updates, based on user feedback. <br/>
<br/>
Elsewhere in <em>this</em> update though, Halide 3.2 adds two new film simulations, or photographic looks: Dawn and Quicksilver. Dawn features "filmic warmth in mid-tones and highlights, paired with rich yet gentle shadows and subtle complementary cool colors." <br/>
<br/>
Quicksilver is described as a "high contrast and muted color" that simulates a "silver-retention alternative process." Halide's developers call it an "excellent midpoint between color and stark black-and-white."<br/>
<br/>
Lastly, version 3.2 introduces a new 16:9 aspect ratio shooting mode for those who want to use a wider framing.<br/>
